# FLARE vs RF — cohort confusion matrix

**What this is.** This page compares two tools that both assign a
categorical superpop label per sample: the **RF classifier** (the
argmax of its superpop probability vector) and **FLARE** (the argmax
of its global ancestry proportion vector). The cohort table sums the
per-cluster (RF call, FLARE call) counts; each row is the RF call,
each column the FLARE call. Cells carry the cohort-wide count and the
row-normalised recall; diagonals are agreement.

**How to read this chart.** Bright diagonal + high row-recall = FLARE
agrees with the RF call cohort-wide for that superpop. A bright
off-diagonal cell, **outlined in red**, is a systematic disagreement:
samples the RF classifier put in one label are being placed by FLARE
in a different label. Compare row-recall (right margin: diag / row
total) with column-precision (bottom margin: diag / column total). A
label with high recall but low precision means FLARE over-calls it
relative to RF.

